# H1-05｜Receiver Contract
## 05.1 Receiver identity
A receiver record is valid only when it resolves to a named role/principal/agent surface and a target scope. A generic label such as `"implementation team"` is insufficient when multiple receivers could act on different repositories, providers, accounts, or environments.
Minimum receiver facts:
```yaml
receiver_id: stable-principal-or-role-id
receiver_class: IMPLEMENTATION_AGENT | OPERATIONS_RECEIVER | HUMAN_OPERATOR | AUTOMATED_EXECUTION_PLANE
target_scope_refs: [non-empty]
trust_boundary: local | repository | provider | production | physical | financial | identity
capability_refs: [non-empty]
instruction_surface_refs: [non-empty-or-none_reason]
currentness_checked_at: ISO-8601
```
## 05.2 Receiver capability and trust boundary
Receiver capability MUST cover the requested operation, environment, and evidence-return obligations. Possessing a tool or credential does not imply authorization to use it.
The receiver validates:
1. own identity matches `handoff.receiver`;
2. subject ID/version/hash match current bytes/state;
3. required refs resolve;
4. late-bound refs are current;
5. applicable repo/environment instructions were discovered read-only;
6. requested permission/effect class is allowed to be evaluated;
7. rollback/compensation/readback path is executable in principle;
8. evidence-return destination is resolvable;
9. required approval can be validated;
10. no newer supersession/invalidation applies.
## 05.3 Receiver obligations
The receiver MUST:
- reject widened scope;
- stop on the first blocking invariant;
- preserve `sender-ready != receiver-accepted`;
- return structured NACK/HOLD/DENY reasons;
- re-check currentness immediately before first mutation for volatile facts;
- execute only actions enumerated by matching ADMIT;
- return subject-bound evidence after attempt;
- observe/read back world effects rather than infer them from provider success.

